Reviewed all 8 units against the semantic-linefeeds rule (sentence boundaries only, list-item commas and compound-predicate conjunctions are not boundaries, rejoin-before-split for paired fused+wrap findings, and only add an extra long-line split when the rejoined remainder actually exceeds ~120 chars at a real boundary).

Key judgment calls:
- **Unit 1**: only `c10` splits purely at the sentence boundary without touching the list-item commas in "types, funcs, or methods."
- **Unit 4**: none of the candidates fully separate all three merged sentences ("for numbers." / "(Why...?" / "Proper typesetting..."); every candidate leaves at least two of them fused, so all are rejected and the correct split is reported under `missing`.
- **Unit 5**: the rejoined remainder is 121 chars, just over the limit, with a real semicolon boundary near the limit and a valid "and" boundary further back — accepted both plus the no-further-split option, chose the semicolon split as closest to the scan-from-limit algorithm.
- **Unit 2/7/8**: rejoin-then-split-once suffices since remainders stay well under 120 chars; extra splits at "which"/comma boundaries in those units are unnecessary over-repair.

